 bigger every day without anyone adding food. Students wonder how they ca\\\\u2026\",\\n      \"id\": \"anchor-1\"\\n    },\\n    \"grade_level\": \"Grade 4\",\\n    \"must_not_introduce\": [\\n      \"chloroplast organelles\",\\n      \"chemical equations of photosynthesis (e.g., 6CO\\\\u2082 + 6H\\\\u2082O \\\\u2192 C\\\\u2086H\\\\u2081\\\\u2082O\\\\u2086 + 6O\\\\u2082)\",\\n      \"glucose as a specific sugar\",\\n      \"light-dependent and light-independent reactions\",\\n      \"limiting factors of photosynthesis\"\\n    ],\\n    \"objective\": \"Recognize that plants are producers that make their own food\",\\n    \"subject\": \"Science\"\\n  },\\n  \"neighbours\": {\\n    \"after\": \"Define \\'producer\\': a living thing that makes its own food. State that plants are producers because they make their own food through photosynthesis. Contrast with animals, which must eat other living things. Reinforce that the garden plants are producers.\",\\n    \"before\": \"Identify the leaf as the food-making factory. Point to a leaf in the garden example. State that inside the leaf there is a special green substance called chlorophyll that catches light. Mention that water enters through roots and travels up the stem to the leaves, and air (carbon dioxide) enters through tiny pores on the leaf. Do not discuss chloroplasts.\"\\n  },\\n  \"section_context\": {\\n    \"section_id\": \"explain\"\\n  },\\n  \"terminology\": [],\\n  \"writer_contract\": {\\n    \"capacity\": {\\n      \"paragraphsMax\": 4,\\n      \"paragraphsMin\": 1,\\n      \"wordsPerParagraph\": 120\\n    },\\n    \"catalogue_version\": \"1.1.0\",\\n    \"content_schema\": {\\n      \"paragraphs\": \"RichParagraph[]\"\\n    },\\n    \"failure_examples\": [],\\n    \"generation_guidance\": \"\",\\n    \"object_id\": \"prose\",\\n    \"object_validation\": []\\n  }\\n}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': \"Return JSON only for this block's content schema.\"}], 'model': 'deepseek-v4-pro', 'max_completion_tokens'